WEBVTT

00:00.870 --> 00:01.940
- [Instructor] What we're
going to do in this video

00:01.940 --> 00:05.040
is think about a way to measure angles.

00:05.040 --> 00:07.010
And there's several ways to do this.

00:07.010 --> 00:08.410
You might have seen this

00:08.410 --> 00:11.500
leveraging things like
degrees in other videos,

00:11.500 --> 00:13.440
but now we're going to
introduce a new concept.

00:13.440 --> 00:14.830
Or maybe you know this concept,

00:14.830 --> 00:17.190
another way of looking at this concept.

00:17.190 --> 00:20.510
So we have this angle ABC

00:20.510 --> 00:22.210
and we want to think about what is a way

00:22.210 --> 00:24.903
to figure out a measure of angle ABC.

00:26.010 --> 00:28.010
Now one way to think about it would be,

00:28.010 --> 00:31.920
well, this angle subtends some arc.

00:31.920 --> 00:36.920
In this case it subtends arc AC.

00:37.500 --> 00:40.670
And we could see if
this angle were smaller,

00:40.670 --> 00:45.280
if its measure were smaller,
it would subtend a smaller arc.

00:45.280 --> 00:47.500
The length of that arc would be smaller.

00:47.500 --> 00:51.530
And if the angle were wider
or had a larger measure,

00:51.530 --> 00:52.910
if it looked something like that,

00:52.910 --> 00:55.520
then the arc length would be larger.

00:55.520 --> 00:59.620
So should we define the
measure of an angle like this

00:59.620 --> 01:04.620
as being equal to the length
of the arc that it subtends?

01:07.030 --> 01:09.190
Is this a good measure?

01:09.190 --> 01:12.160
Well some of you might immediately
see a problem with that

01:12.160 --> 01:13.730
because this length,

01:13.730 --> 01:16.340
the length of the arc that is subtended,

01:16.340 --> 01:18.770
is not just dependent on the angle,

01:18.770 --> 01:20.000
the measure of the angle,

01:20.000 --> 01:22.680
it also depends on how big of
a circle you're dealing with.

01:22.680 --> 01:24.220
If the radius is larger

01:24.220 --> 01:26.480
then you're gonna have
a larger arc length.

01:26.480 --> 01:29.743
For example, let me introduce
another circle here.

01:30.900 --> 01:33.490
So we have the same angle measure,

01:33.490 --> 01:35.110
the central angle right over here,

01:35.110 --> 01:37.620
you could say angle ABC is still the same,

01:37.620 --> 01:40.280
but now it subtends a different arc

01:40.280 --> 01:42.070
in these two different circles.

01:42.070 --> 01:45.230
You have this arc right over here.

01:45.230 --> 01:48.283
Let's call this arc DE,

01:49.630 --> 01:53.220
and the length of arc DE

01:54.210 --> 01:58.670
is not equal to the length of AC.

01:58.670 --> 02:00.510
And so we can't measure an angle

02:00.510 --> 02:03.840
just by the length of
the arc that it subtends

02:03.840 --> 02:06.620
if that angle is a
central angle in a circle.

02:06.620 --> 02:08.950
So we can get rid of that equality here.

02:08.950 --> 02:11.040
But what could we do?

02:11.040 --> 02:14.020
Well you might realize that these two pis

02:14.020 --> 02:15.070
that I just created,

02:15.070 --> 02:19.760
you could kind of say pi ABC and pi DBE,

02:19.760 --> 02:21.930
these are similar pis.

02:21.930 --> 02:24.770
Now we're not used to talking
in terms of similar pis,

02:24.770 --> 02:26.450
but what does it mean to be similar?

02:26.450 --> 02:30.320
Well, you have similarity
if you can map one thing

02:30.320 --> 02:33.190
on to another, one shape on to another,

02:33.190 --> 02:35.440
through not just rigid transformations,

02:35.440 --> 02:37.550
but also through dilations.

02:37.550 --> 02:40.223
And in this situation, if
you were to take pi ABC

02:41.380 --> 02:44.880
and just dilate it by a
scale factor larger than one

02:44.880 --> 02:47.480
there's some scale factor
where you would dilate it out

02:47.480 --> 02:49.393
to pi DBE.

02:50.690 --> 02:52.800
And what's interesting about that is

02:52.800 --> 02:54.340
if two things are similar

02:54.340 --> 02:56.890
that means the ratio
between corresponding parts

02:56.890 --> 02:58.690
are going to be the same.

02:58.690 --> 03:03.203
So for example, the ratio
of the length of arc AC

03:05.960 --> 03:10.630
to segment, the length of segment BC

03:10.630 --> 03:15.630
is going to be equal to
the ratio of the length

03:15.830 --> 03:20.830
of arc DE to the length of segment BE.

03:23.774 --> 03:28.774
So maybe this is a good measure

03:29.760 --> 03:31.610
for an angle.

03:31.610 --> 03:34.540
And it is indeed a measure that we use

03:34.540 --> 03:38.130
in geometry and trigonometry
and throughout mathematics

03:38.130 --> 03:43.130
and we call it the radian
measure of an angle.

03:47.856 --> 03:49.833
And it equals the ratio

03:55.340 --> 04:00.340
of the arc length subtended

04:03.420 --> 04:06.020
by that angle to the radius.

04:11.810 --> 04:15.500
We just saw that in both
of these situations.

04:15.500 --> 04:18.300
So let's see if we can make
this a little bit more tangible.

04:18.300 --> 04:21.130
Let's say we had a circle here

04:22.480 --> 04:24.020
and it has a central point.

04:24.020 --> 04:27.240
Let's just call that
point, I don't know, F.

04:27.240 --> 04:29.853
And then let me create an angle here.

04:30.840 --> 04:33.460
And actually I could make a right angle.

04:33.460 --> 04:34.920
So let's call that F.

04:34.920 --> 04:36.360
And let's see, call this point G,

04:36.360 --> 04:38.260
and let's call this H.

04:38.260 --> 04:43.080
And let's say that the radius
over here is two meters.

04:43.080 --> 04:46.920
And now what would be the
length of the arc subtended

04:46.920 --> 04:48.683
by angle GFH?

04:50.550 --> 04:53.010
Well this could even be
one 1/4 the circumference

04:53.010 --> 04:55.480
of this entire circle, the
way that I've drawn it.

04:55.480 --> 04:57.460
So the entire circumference,

04:57.460 --> 04:58.293
you could write it here,

04:58.293 --> 04:59.200
the circumference is going to be equal

04:59.200 --> 05:03.040
to 2 pi times the radius,

05:03.040 --> 05:04.463
which is going to be
two pi times two meters

05:04.463 --> 05:08.050
so it's going to be four pi meters.

05:08.050 --> 05:12.310
And so if this arc length is 1/4 of that

05:12.310 --> 05:15.403
this is going to be pi meters.

05:16.450 --> 05:20.870
And so based on this arc
length and this radius,

05:20.870 --> 05:25.333
what is going to be the measure
of angle GFH in radians?

05:28.750 --> 05:33.750
Well we could say the measure
of angle GFH in radians

05:33.780 --> 05:35.250
is gonna be the ratio

05:35.250 --> 05:39.870
between the length of the
arc subtended and the radius.

05:39.870 --> 05:44.870
And so it's going to be
pi meters over two meters.

05:47.100 --> 05:50.130
The meters, you could view
those as canceling out.

05:50.130 --> 05:52.490
Which equals pi over two,

05:52.490 --> 05:54.120
and pi over two what?

05:54.120 --> 05:59.120
Well we would say this is
equal to pi over two radians!

05:59.290 --> 06:03.870
Now one thing to think about
is why do we call it radians.

06:03.870 --> 06:06.600
It seems close to the word radius.

06:06.600 --> 06:10.100
And one way to think about it
is when you divide this length

06:10.100 --> 06:12.310
by the length of the radius

06:12.310 --> 06:15.280
you're figuring out how many
of the radii is equivalent

06:15.280 --> 06:17.400
to the arc length in question.

06:17.400 --> 06:21.690
So, for example, in
this situation one radii

06:21.690 --> 06:22.930
would look something like this.

06:22.930 --> 06:24.380
If you took the same length

06:24.380 --> 06:26.160
and you just went around like this,

06:26.160 --> 06:29.840
so you can see it's going to
be one point something radii.

06:29.840 --> 06:31.170
And that's why you could also say

06:31.170 --> 06:33.150
it's one point something radians.

06:33.150 --> 06:34.880
If you took pi divided by two

06:34.880 --> 06:36.480
you're going to get a little bit over one.

06:36.480 --> 06:38.320
You're gonna get one point, I don't know,

06:38.320 --> 06:39.753
O, seven, something.
